# Auth Edge Runtime Safety

`lib/auth.ts` is imported by `middleware.ts`, which runs in the **Next.js Edge runtime**. The Edge runtime does NOT support Node.js APIs (TCP sockets, `fs`, `net`, `tls`, etc.).

## Hard Rule

**`lib/auth.ts` must NEVER import any module that depends on Node.js APIs.**

Banned import chains include (but are not limited to):
- `weaviate-client` (TCP/TLS connections)
- `lib/weaviate.ts` (wraps weaviate-client)
- `lib/users.ts` (imports weaviate-client)
- `lib/audit.ts` (imports weaviate-client)
- `lib/permissions.ts` → `lib/permission-sets.ts` (imports weaviate-client)
- Any `lib/*.ts` file that calls `withWeaviate()`

## Architecture

| File | Runtime | What it exports |
|---|---|---|
| `lib/auth.ts` | Edge-safe | `auth`, `handlers`, `signIn`, `signOut` (NextAuth core) |
| `lib/auth-server.ts` | Node.js only | `requireAuth`, `requireRole`, `requirePermission`, `getCurrentUser` |

- **Middleware** (`middleware.ts`) imports from `lib/auth.ts` only.
- **API routes** import from `lib/auth-server.ts` for auth helpers that need Weaviate.
- **`lib/auth-server.ts`** may import anything (Weaviate, audit, users, permissions) because API routes run in Node.js.

## Before Editing `lib/auth.ts`

1. Check whether the new import depends on Node.js APIs (directly or transitively).
2. If it does, put the logic in `lib/auth-server.ts` instead.
3. If you need to add functionality to NextAuth callbacks (like audit logging), use a server-side hook in `lib/auth-server.ts` rather than the NextAuth config.
